package cajeta.io.net.http;

import cajeta.lang.String;

/**
 * Thrown by the incremental HTTP parser ([`HttpParser`](HttpParser.cajeta),
 * NET-7.3) when the byte stream **ends before a complete message head
 * was seen** — the peer closed (or the producer signalled end-of-input
 * via [`endInput`](HttpParser.cajeta)) while the parser was still
 * waiting for the blank-line head terminator (`CRLFCRLF`). This is the
 * failure the `missing-crlf-terminator` abuse vector pins: a head that
 * never terminates must surface as a clean `UnexpectedEof`, not an
 * infinite wait.
 *
 * It is **not** raised on a normal chunk boundary — the incremental
 * parser is explicitly resumable across `feed` calls, so a partial
 * head simply means "feed me more." `UnexpectedEof` is reserved for
 * the terminal case the caller asserts with `endInput()`.
 *
 * ## Hierarchy
 *
 * As with the sibling HTTP parse exceptions, `UnexpectedEof` lives
 * under `cajeta.io.net.NetException` per the spec (plan item **NET-7.7**).
 * That item has now landed: this descends from the intermediate
 * [`HttpException`](HttpException.cajeta) → `cajeta.io.net.NetException`
 * root, so it is catchable as `HttpException` (every HTTP parse fault)
 * or the `NetException` root. (NET-7.3 originally rooted it on
 * `cajeta.error.RecoverableException` as a placeholder; NET-7.7 is the
 * one-line reparent.) The `(message, bytesBuffered)` constructor
 * contract stays **identical** across the reparent.
 *
 * ## The inherited `kind`
 *
 * As a `NetException`, every instance carries the cross-platform
 * [`kind`](../NetException.cajeta) ordinal, classified to
 * [`NetException.KIND_INVALID`](../NetException.cajeta) (12) — the whole
 * HTTP family's bucket — since a truncated message is an invalid-input
 * protocol fault, not an errno-derived socket error. (A transport-level
 * socket close surfaces separately as a `ConnectionResetException` /
 * `BrokenPipeException`; `UnexpectedEof` is the *protocol* reading that
 * the bytes received did not form a complete head.) The count of head
 * bytes seen still rides on `bytesBuffered`.
 */
public class UnexpectedEofException extends HttpException {
    /**
     * Number of head bytes the parser had accumulated when the input
     * ended without a head terminator.
     */
    public int64 bytesBuffered;

    public UnexpectedEofException(String message, int64 bytesBuffered) {
        this.message = message;
        this.cause = 0;
        this.kind = 12;   // NetException.KIND_INVALID (via HttpException)
        this.bytesBuffered = bytesBuffered;
    }
}
